<body>

<h1>&#128214; Full Code Explanation &mdash; MongoDB Form Submission in Next.js</h1>

<p>This document explains <b>every file</b> in your project that is involved in saving form data to MongoDB. We will go through each file one by one, line by line.</p>

<h2>&#128194; Project Structure (Only the relevant files)</h2>

<pre>
app/
&#9500;&#9472;&#9472; Details.tsx              &larr; The Form (Frontend - what user sees)
&#9500;&#9472;&#9472; lib/
&#9474;   &#9500;&#9472;&#9472; mongodb.ts           &larr; Database Connection
&#9474;   &#9492;&#9472;&#9472; model/
&#9474;       &#9492;&#9472;&#9472; user.ts          &larr; Database Schema (shape of your data)
&#9492;&#9472;&#9472; api/
    &#9492;&#9472;&#9472; user/
        &#9492;&#9472;&#9472; route.ts         &larr; API Route (Backend - handles form submission)
</pre>

<h2>&#128200; How the Data Flows</h2>

<p>When a user clicks "Submit", here is what happens step by step:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Step</th><th>What Happens</th><th>Where</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>User fills in name, number, email and clicks Submit</td><td>Details.tsx (Browser)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Form validates the input (checks blank, 10 digits, @ symbol)</td><td>Details.tsx (Browser)</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Form sends a POST request with JSON data to /api/user</td><td>Details.tsx &rarr; route.ts</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>API route connects to MongoDB</td><td>route.ts &rarr; mongodb.ts</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>API route creates a new User document and saves it</td><td>route.ts &rarr; user.ts &rarr; MongoDB</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>API sends a success/error response back</td><td>route.ts &rarr; Details.tsx</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Form shows an alert and clears the fields</td><td>Details.tsx (Browser)</td></tr>
</table>

<br/><br/>

<h2>File 1: <code>app/lib/mongodb.ts</code> &mdash; Database Connection</h2>

<p>This file is responsible for <b>connecting your app to the MongoDB database</b>.</p>

<pre>
import mongoose from 'mongoose'
</pre>
<p><b>mongoose</b> is a library that makes it easy to work with MongoDB from Node.js/TypeScript. Instead of writing raw MongoDB queries, Mongoose gives you a cleaner way to define data models and interact with the database.</p>

<pre>
const mongodb = async () =&gt; {
</pre>
<p>We create an <b>async function</b> called <code>mongodb</code>. It's <code>async</code> because connecting to a database takes time (it's a network operation), so we need to <b>wait</b> for it.</p>

<pre>
    try {
        await mongoose.connect('mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/Details');
        console.log('database connected sucess');
    }
</pre>

<p><b>try</b> block: We attempt to connect to MongoDB.</p>
<p><b>mongoose.connect(...)</b>: This is the actual connection command. Let's break down the URL:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Part</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>mongodb://</code></td><td>The protocol (like https:// for websites)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>127.0.0.1</code></td><td>Localhost &mdash; means your own computer</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>:27017</code></td><td>The port number where MongoDB runs (27017 is the default)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>/Details</code></td><td>The database name. If it doesn't exist, MongoDB auto-creates it on first insert</td></tr>
</table>

<p><b>await</b>: We wait for the connection to finish before moving on. If successful, it prints "database connected sucess" in the terminal.</p>

<pre>
    catch(err) {
        console.log("Database error ", err)
    }
</pre>
<p><b>catch</b> block: If the connection fails (e.g., MongoDB is not running), the error is caught here and printed to the terminal instead of crashing the app.</p>

<pre>
export default mongodb
</pre>
<p>We <b>export</b> the function so other files can import and use it.</p>

<br/>

<h2>File 2: <code>app/lib/model/user.ts</code> &mdash; Database Schema/Model</h2>

<p>This file defines the <b>shape (structure)</b> of the data that will be stored in MongoDB.</p>

<div class="note"><b>Think of it like this:</b> A schema is like a <b>form template</b>. It says "every user entry MUST have a name, a number, and an email."</div>

<pre>
import mongoose, { mongo } from "mongoose";
</pre>
<p>Imports mongoose to use its Schema and Model features.</p>

<pre>
const UserSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
    name:   { type: String,  required: true },
    number: { type: Number,  required: true },
    email:  { type: String,  required: true },
});
</pre>

<p><b>new mongoose.Schema({...})</b>: Creates a new schema (blueprint). Each field is defined with:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Required</th><th>What it stores</th></tr>
<tr><td>name</td><td>String</td><td>&#9989; Yes</td><td>The user's name</td></tr>
<tr><td>number</td><td>Number</td><td>&#9989; Yes</td><td>The user's phone number</td></tr>
<tr><td>email</td><td>String</td><td>&#9989; Yes</td><td>The user's email</td></tr>
</table>

<pre>
const User = mongoose.models.user || mongoose.model("user", UserSchema);
</pre>

<p>This line is <b>very important</b>. Let's break it down:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>mongoose.model("user", UserSchema)</b>: Creates a model called "user" based on the schema. This tells Mongoose: "Create a <b>collection</b> called <code>users</code> in MongoDB (it adds an 's' automatically) and every document inside it must follow UserSchema."</li>
<li><b>mongoose.models.user ||</b>: This is a <b>safety check for Next.js</b>. Next.js uses hot reloading during development &mdash; when you save a file, it reloads the code. Without this check, Mongoose would try to create the same model again and throw an error: "Cannot overwrite 'user' model once compiled". So we first check: "Does this model already exist?" If yes, reuse it. If no, create it.</li>
</ul>

<pre>
export default User;
</pre>
<p>Export the model so the API route can use it to save data.</p>

<br/>

<h2>File 3: <code>app/api/user/route.ts</code> &mdash; API Route (Backend)</h2>

<p>This is the <b>backend</b> of your app. When the form sends data, it arrives here. This file processes it and saves it to MongoDB.</p>

<div class="important"><b>In Next.js</b>, any file placed inside <code>app/api/</code> becomes an API endpoint. The folder structure determines the URL:<br/>
<code>app/api/user/route.ts</code> &rarr; accessible at <code>http://localhost:3000/api/user</code></div>

<pre>
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from "next/server";
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>NextRequest</b>: Represents the incoming request (the data sent from the form)</li>
<li><b>NextResponse</b>: Used to send a response back to the frontend</li>
</ul>

<pre>
import mongodb from "@/app/lib/mongodb";
</pre>
<p>Imports the database connection function. <b>@/</b> is an alias for the project root.</p>

<pre>
import User from "@/app/lib/model/user";
</pre>
<p>Imports the User model (the blueprint) we defined earlier.</p>

<pre>
export const POST = async (req: Request) =&gt; {
</pre>
<p>In Next.js App Router, the function name defines which <b>HTTP method</b> it handles:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>POST</code> &rarr; handles POST requests (used for <b>sending/creating</b> data)</li>
<li><code>GET</code> &rarr; handles GET requests (used for <b>fetching</b> data)</li>
</ul>

<pre>
    await mongodb();
</pre>
<p><b>Connects to the database</b> before doing anything else. If the connection already exists, Mongoose will reuse it.</p>

<pre>
    const data = await req.json();
</pre>
<p><b>req.json()</b>: Extracts the JSON body from the request. After this line, <code>data</code> contains the object like: <code>{ name: "John", number: "1234567890", email: "john@gmail.com" }</code></p>

<pre>
    const user = new User({
        name: data.name,
        number: data.number,
        email: data.email,
    });
</pre>
<p><b>new User({...})</b>: Creates a new document (entry) using the User model. This is like filling out the form template with actual values. At this point, the data is <b>NOT yet saved</b> to the database &mdash; it's just created in memory.</p>

<pre>
    await user.save();
</pre>
<p><b>user.save()</b>: This is the line that actually <b>saves the data to MongoDB</b>. <code>await</code> ensures we wait for it to finish.</p>

<pre>
    return NextResponse.json({
        message: "user added successfully",
        user,
    });
</pre>
<p>Sends a <b>JSON response</b> back to the frontend with a success message and the saved user data (including the auto-generated <code>_id</code> from MongoDB).</p>

<br/>

<h2>File 4: <code>app/Details.tsx</code> &mdash; The Form (Frontend)</h2>

<p>This is what the user sees and interacts with in the browser.</p>

<pre>
"use client"
</pre>
<p><b>Very important in Next.js!</b> By default, Next.js components run on the server. But this component uses <code>useState</code> (React state) and handles button clicks, which require the browser (client). <code>"use client"</code> tells Next.js: "Run this component in the browser."</p>

<pre>
import React, { useState } from 'react'
</pre>
<p><b>useState</b>: A React Hook that lets you store and update data that can change (like form inputs).</p>

<pre>
const [name, setName] = useState('');
const [number, setNumber] = useState('');
const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
</pre>

<p>Creates <b>3 state variables</b> to store the form values:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Variable</th><th>Setter Function</th><th>Initial Value</th><th>What it stores</th></tr>
<tr><td>name</td><td>setName</td><td>'' (empty)</td><td>User's typed name</td></tr>
<tr><td>number</td><td>setNumber</td><td>'' (empty)</td><td>User's typed number</td></tr>
<tr><td>email</td><td>setEmail</td><td>'' (empty)</td><td>User's typed email</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The handleSubmit Function</h3>

<pre>
const handleSubmit = async () =&gt; {
</pre>
<p>Called when the user clicks the Submit button. <code>async</code> because we use <code>await</code> for the API call.</p>

<h3>Validation (Checking the data before sending):</h3>

<pre>
if (name === "") {
    alert("Dont keep it blank")
}
else if (number.length !== 10) {
    alert("Number should be of 10 digit")
}
else if (!email.includes("@")) {
    alert("email should be valid")
}
</pre>

<table>
<tr><th>Check</th><th>What it validates</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>name === ""</code></td><td>Name must not be empty</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>number.length !== 10</code></td><td>Phone number must be exactly 10 digits</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>!email.includes("@")</code></td><td>Email must contain @ symbol</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Sending Data to the API:</h3>

<pre>
const res = await fetch("/api/user", {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { "content-Type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify({ name, number, email }),
});
</pre>

<p><b>fetch("/api/user", {...})</b>: Sends an HTTP request to your API route.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Property</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>"/api/user"</code></td><td>The URL of your API (maps to app/api/user/route.ts)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>method: 'POST'</code></td><td>We're sending data (not fetching)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>headers</code></td><td>Tells the server the body is in JSON format</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>body: JSON.stringify({...})</code></td><td>Converts the JS object to a JSON string for sending</td></tr>
</table>

<pre>
const data = await res.json();
</pre>
<p>Reads the response from the API and converts it from JSON to a JavaScript object.</p>

<pre>
if (res.ok) {
    alert("details added successfully");
    setName("");
    setNumber("");
    setEmail("");
}
</pre>
<p><b>res.ok</b>: true if HTTP status is 200-299 (success). If successful, shows a success alert and clears all the input fields.</p>

<h3>The Input Fields (JSX):</h3>

<pre>
&lt;input type="text"
    value={name}
    onChange={(e) =&gt; setName(e.target.value)}
/&gt;
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>value={name}</b>: The input always shows the current value of name state</li>
<li><b>onChange={(e) =&gt; setName(e.target.value)}</b>: Every time the user types, it updates the name state</li>
<li>This pattern is called a <b>Controlled Component</b> &mdash; React controls the input value through state</li>
</ul>

<br/>

<h2>&#128273; Key Concepts Summary</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
<tr><td>"use client"</td><td>Tells Next.js this component runs in the browser</td></tr>
<tr><td>useState</td><td>React hook to store data that can change</td></tr>
<tr><td>mongoose.Schema</td><td>Defines the structure/rules for your data</td></tr>
<tr><td>mongoose.model</td><td>Creates a "collection manager" based on the schema</td></tr>
<tr><td>mongoose.connect</td><td>Establishes connection to MongoDB</td></tr>
<tr><td>fetch()</td><td>Sends HTTP requests from the browser to the API</td></tr>
<tr><td>req.json()</td><td>Reads the JSON body from an incoming request</td></tr>
<tr><td>user.save()</td><td>Saves a document to MongoDB</td></tr>
<tr><td>NextResponse.json()</td><td>Sends a JSON response from the API</td></tr>
<tr><td>API Route</td><td>Backend endpoint created by placing route.ts inside app/api/</td></tr>
</table>

<br/>

<h2>&#128452; What Happens in MongoDB</h2>

<p>After successful submission, your data is stored in:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Database</b>: Details</li>
<li><b>Collection</b>: users (Mongoose adds 's' to model name "user")</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Example saved document:</b></p>
<pre>
{
    "_id": "664cba1f2e3a4b5c6d7e8f9a",
    "name": "John",
    "number": 1234567890,
    "email": "john@gmail.com",
    "__v": 0
}
</pre>

<ul>
<li><b>_id</b>: Auto-generated unique ID by MongoDB</li>
<li><b>__v</b>: Version key added by Mongoose (used for internal tracking)</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>
